  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"I found an interesting Sanskrit song, and I turned it into a poem of my own, where I played with a central spine like the spine of a horse looking down on it. Each part of the body of the horse is related to different parts of the universe, like its piss is rain, its bones are the stars, and so on. I reworked the song into a kind of concrete poem, trying to make the centre like a spine so it ranged left and right, and I alternated the parts of the horse and the parts of the universe, to make them more rythmic ... I decided to convert the poem into a book; it was the first book that I printed on my own press"--The word returned.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An upside-down book. The poem is printed in blue/black when read in one direction, and in orange in the other.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Bound in orange and blue publisher's cloth; in a slipcase.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">An edition of 25.</subfield>
  </datafield>
